@tableofcontents


This benchmark solves the single black hole (Schwarzschild black hole)
with ExaGRyPE.


## Benchmark sketch for non-specialists


To understand what is going on here, it might help to read through @ref page_exagrype first.
According to the description there, ExaGRyPE solves a wave equation, i.e. it models how the effects of mass ripple through spacetime.

In this benchmark, however, we solve for a stationary black hole. Such an object does not really exist
(real black holes rotate, accrete matter, and therefore grow), but it provides a good test
case for our numerical methods, not least because plenty of reference data and analytical solutions are available.

At the centre of the simulation domain, we have a singularity, i.e. a right-hand-side term
that represents a stationary distortion of spacetime.
It is chosen such that it injects just enough change into spacetime at the centre to counteract
the waves travelling out through the boundary of the simulation domain.

You can think of this as similar to a guitar string that we pluck in the middle while holding it tight.
Normally, the string would vibrate, but here we pluck it in just the right way to counteract the vibration,
so everything becomes stationary.

While the output is not particularly exciting (the simulation should eventually yield a
stationary solution), the problem is numerically challenging. Boundary-condition effects,
discretisation errors, adaptive mesh refinement effects, and other numerical artefacts all
feed into a strongly nonlinear equation system that can easily blow up and become unstable.
Solving a stationary setup with a time-dependent solver is therefore an excellent stress test.

@image html benchmarks/exagrype/single-black-hole/illustration-of-principle.png 

<div style="background-color: #cfc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
  Before benchmarking this setup, we recommend ensuring that
  @ref benchmarks_exagrype_kernel_benchmarks and
  @ref benchmarks_exagrype_gauge_wave complete successfully.
  The former assesses the computational efficiency of various kernel variants across
  different platforms, while the latter validates the numerical accuracy of the
  overall solver assembly, including its convergence orders.
</div>


## Benchmark parameters

### Time duration

The single black hole benchmark needs some time to burn in and reach a stationary state. 
After that, we have to evolve it long enough to be reasonably confident that there are no tiny little wiggles in the outcome that eventually could blow up.
Indeed, sometimes we have small errors that travel towards the domain boundary.
If they are not properly absorbed there, they can reflect back into the domain.
As they travel forth and back and we inject more and more error, the simulation blows up.
So we have to evolve the whole setup long enough.
We go beyond what most papers do at ask for t=100.


### Domain size

If we pick the domain too small, we will almost certainly mess up the boundary conditions.
This setup employs Sommerfeld conditions, which are designed to absorb outgoing waves and should not reflect them back into the domain.
However, they are only an approximation, and they are only valid if the waves are far enough away from the boundary.
There will always be some reflection, and the smaller the domain, the stronger the reflection.
So we pick a @f$ [486,486,486]^3 @f$ domain offset by @f$ [-243,-243,-243] @f$ so its centre is at the origin, which is where the singularity (black hole) is located.


### Mesh size

The maximum mesh size is a mesh spacing (volume size) of 

              volume_size = 0.4

for Finite Difference and Finite Volume schemes. For DG, you can allow a mesh size of 4.0,
but at least fourth-order spatial resolution is needed.


### Time stepping

As it is a stationary setup, we don't know what time stepping scheme is mandatory. 
One would at least expect the order to match the spatial order (so usually 4th order Runge-Kutta),
but maybe a lower order scheme is sufficient. 
As starting point, we recommend to use the same time stepping scheme as in the gauge wave benchmark, i.e. 4th order Runge-Kutta.


### Boundary conditions

Sommerfeld boundary conditions are used, which are designed to absorb outgoing waves and should not reflect them back into the domain.
Besides these boundary conditions, you might have to use Kreiss-Oliger dissipation if you employ a fourth order scheme.


## Prepare Peano, ExaHyPE and ExaGRyPE

The settings below resemble content discussed in @ref tutorials_exahype2_exagrype_522_single-black-hole.
It makes sense to run through this tutorial and the tutorials before that one, before you start benchmarking the single black hole setup
as discussed here.

<div style="background-color: #ccf ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
  This benchmark requires a third-party tool: You will need the GNU Scientific Library (GSL) installed on your system. 
</div>


## Core build settings

For this single black hole benchmark, the following Peano toolboxes are mandatory:
  
  - blockstructured (```--enable-blockstructured```)
  - exahype (```--enable-exahype```, this switches on ExaHyPE 2)
  - exagrype (```--enable-exagrype```)
  
In theory, the benchmark passes without parallelisation. In practice, it is so 
computationally demanding that we recommend to use multithreading and MPI. Due 
to this, you also need

   - load balancing (```--enable-loadbalancing```)

GPU support is, as always, optional. Other advanced features such as particle 
tracers are not used. However, you will need GSL. GSL is trivial to enable

    --with-gsl
    
but depending on your system, you might have to load the GSL module separately
before you build (and run) the application:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
module load gsl
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

GSL's headers and library have to be found by the compiler (`gsl-config --version` has to work).
If you configure Peano without GSL (see @ref benchmarks_exagrype_single_black_hole_configure), the code generator still works, but
the compiler stops with the message
"exagrype.api.initialvalues.single_black_hole() requires TwoPunctures: configure Peano with --with-gsl".
The initial data comes from TwoPunctures, which is only part of the ExaGRyPE library if GSL was enabled.

@anchor benchmarks_exagrype_single_black_hole_configure
## Configure and build Peano

The benchmark uses the Autotools build (the CMake build works with the analogous `ENABLE_*`/`WITH_*` options).
The following line is what the measurements below used (Intel oneAPI 2024.2 `icpx`, OpenMP, an 8-core workstation):

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
libtoolize; aclocal; autoconf; autoheader; cp src/config.h.in .; automake --add-missing   # only after a fresh clone
./configure CXX=icpx CC=icpx \
            CXXFLAGS="-g -O3 -qopenmp --std=c++23 -Wno-unknown-attributes -Wno-attributes" \
            LDFLAGS="-qopenmp" \
            --enable-exahype --enable-blockstructured --enable-loadbalancing --enable-exagrype \
            --with-multithreading=omp --with-gsl
make clean
make -j
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Some remarks:

- `--enable-exahype --enable-blockstructured --enable-loadbalancing --enable-exagrype --with-gsl` are mandatory, see above.
  `--enable-particles` is not needed.
- `-g` does not slow down the code, but it is required if you want to profile with line information.
- `--with-multithreading=omp` is what the thread counts of the performance study refer to (`OMP_NUM_THREADS`).
  The performance study only makes sense with multithreading.
- If you want to profile with Intel VTune's instrumentation API, add `-I<vtune>/include` to the compiler flags and
  `-L<vtune>/lib64` to the linker flags and `LIBS=-littnotify`. The profiles below did not rely on the API: they use
  VTune's software sampling and need nothing more than `-g`.
- Peano is built into several flavours of each library (release, `_debug`, `_asserts`, `_trace`, `_stats`). The build variants
  of the benchmark pick them: debug links `_debug`, profile links `_stats` and production links the plain release libraries.
  Make sure `make` has completed without errors before you start: all three variants of the benchmark need the libraries of
  ExaHyPE 2 and ExaGRyPE in the flavour you use (`src/exahype2/libExaHyPE2Core3d*.a`, `src/exagrype/libExaGRyPECore3d*.a`).
- The Python side needs the packages of `requirements.txt`: `pip install -e .` in a virtual environment, or
  `export PYTHONPATH=<peano>/src`.
- The scripts of this benchmark generate into their own output directory and call `make` there. Add
  `--peano-directory <path to Peano>` to `single-black-hole.py` if the script is not run from within
  `benchmarks/exagrype/single-black-hole` (the studies script does this for you).

A first test whether everything works:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export PYTHONPATH=<peano>/src
python3 single-black-hole.py --scheme fv --variant profile --end-time 0.2 --output-directory test
cd test && ./single-black-hole
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

builds in about 20 s and finishes after about 4 s on eight cores.


# Run the benchmark

All experiments are driven by single-black-hole.py. It generates, builds
and (together with studies-single-black-hole.py) runs the setup.
The script does not write checkpoints by default, and the studies below do not
need them.


## Build variants

The parameters above (domain 486, volume size 0.4, t=100) describe a run with more than three
billion volumes. This is impossible on a workstation. The script therefore
distinguishes three variants through `--variant`:

| Variant    | Compile mode        | Domain | Volume size | End time | Purpose |
| ---------- | ------------------- | ------ | ----------- | -------- | ------- |
| debug      | Peano debug build   | 18     | 1.2         | 0.5      | Does the code work at all? |
| profile    | Peano stats build   | 54     | 1.2         | 5        | Small enough to profile and to run parameter studies on one node. |
| production | Peano release build | 486    | 0.4         | 100      | The setup from above. |

The variant fixes the defaults of `--domain-size`, `--volume-size` and `--end-time`. You can
override each of them explicitly. Unless you study the numerics of the full domain, work against the profile
variant.


## Adaptive mesh refinement

The maximum mesh size of the README is a hard bound: the mesh must never be coarser than the regular mesh
with `--volume-size`. Adaptive mesh refinement (AMR) therefore does not coarsen the far field. It refines the regular mesh further
around the black hole, where the singularity sits and the solution varies strongest:

    python3 single-black-hole.py --scheme fv --amr-levels 1 --amr-radius 4 --variant profile

adds one level (factor three, i.e. volumes three times smaller per axis) to every cell within
distance 4 of the puncture. The criterion depends on the position only and never asks for
an erase; the mesh is static. As the time step size of the explicit schemes scales with the
smallest volume, one level makes the time step three times smaller.

<div style="background-color: #ffc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
  The refined region is considerably larger than the radius suggests. On the profile mesh (base cells of width 6),
  a radius of 1 already refines a block of 5x5x5 base cells (3375 fine cells next to 604 coarse cells, i.e. a 30 wide
  cube around the puncture), and a radius of 4 yields 7879 cells on the single rank (729 without AMR).
  We have not yet analysed where this additional refinement comes from (the criterion only refines cells that
  contain a volume closer than the radius); the cell counts are printed in the step report of every run
  (`min mesh size=..., #local cells=...`). Take them into account when you choose `--amr-levels`: one level
  costs a factor of 14 (radius 1), 25 (radius 4, one thread) or 40 (default radius 12, eight threads) in runtime on the profile mesh,
  two levels did not fit into the 15 GB of our workstation (11 GB with a single thread, and hours of runtime).
  The default radius of 12 refines a block that is far too big for the profile variant. Use `--amr-radius 1` or `4` there.
</div>

<div style="background-color: #fcc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
  AMR is available for the Finite Volumes (`--scheme fv`) only for the time being. 
  The script refuses `--amr-levels` in combination with the Finite Differences. 
</div>


## Study of numerical schemes

The script studies-single-black-hole.py with the argument `schemes` builds and runs

| Name | Scheme |
| ---- | ------ |
| fv-regular, fv-amr-1 | Finite Volumes (Rusanov, explicit Euler) on the regular mesh and with one additional level (radius 1) |
| fd4-rk2, fd4-rk3, fd4-rk4 | Fourth-order Finite Differences with Runge-Kutta of order 2, 3 and 4 |
| fd4-rk4-lopsided | fd4-rk4 plus lop-sided advection along the shift |
| fd4-rk4-lopsided-ko | fd4-rk4-lopsided plus Kreiss-Oliger dissipation (sigma=8) |

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 studies-single-black-hole.py schemes --variant profile --work-directory studies-schemes
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Each configuration is built into its own subdirectory; the results land in
`studies-schemes/results-schemes.csv` (exit code and wall time per run) next to the console
log of every run. As the README explains, the stationary solution is a stress test: a scheme
that is not stable ends with a non-zero exit code or aborts early.

### Outcome on the profile mesh

All numbers stem from an eight-core workstation, the profile variant (domain 54, h=1, t=5), eight OpenMP threads, and a
Peano build as described above. "Sweeps" is the number of mesh traversals of the time stepping, i.e. the number of time steps
(the time step size follows the CFL condition).

| Scheme | Wall time [s] | Sweeps | Time step size dt |
| ------ | ------------: | -----: | ----------------: |
| fv-regular | 9.3 | 35 | 0.15 |
| fv-amr-1 (radius 1) | 174 | 102 | 0.05 |
| fd4-rk2 | 31 | 64 | 0.083 |
| fd4-rk3 | 47 | 64 | 0.083 |
| fd4-rk4 | 62 | 64 | 0.083 |
| fd4-rk4-lopsided | 82 | 64 | 0.083 |
| fd4-rk4-lopsided-ko | 82 | 62 | 0.083 |

(fv-amr-1 and the two lop-sided runs use `--compiler sequential`, see the warning below. The other runs use the default
compiler `fusion`.)

What we learn from this:

- None of the schemes blows up or produces NaNs within t=5. The study therefore does not separate stable from unstable schemes
  yet. This is no evidence for long-time stability: the README asks for t=100 on the full domain, where reflections from the boundary
  have time to come back. Without checkpoints, we also cannot say anything about the accuracy of the schemes, only about their cost.
  Run the study with `--variant production --end-time 100` (and checkpoints) on a cluster for that.
- Per time step, the Finite Differences are 2 to 5 times more expensive than the Finite Volumes
  (fd4-rk2: 0.44 s, fd4-rk4: 0.92 s per sweep, fv-regular: 0.19 s per sweep; all with eight threads). The Runge-Kutta order drives the cost
  almost linearly (2 stages: 31 s, 3: 47 s, 4: 62 s). The time step size of the Finite Differences is 0.083, i.e. almost half of the
  Finite Volumes' one, so the Finite Differences need 64 instead of 35 sweeps. Whether an RK order below 4 suffices
  for a stationary solution is a question of accuracy, which this study cannot answer.
- The lop-sided advection costs roughly another 30% over plain fd4-rk4 (82 s vs. 62 s, however measured with different compilers). The Kreiss-Oliger dissipation is free in comparison (82 s in both cases).
- One level of adaptive mesh refinement around the puncture makes the Finite Volumes about 14 times slower (172 s vs. 12.1 s with the
  sequential compiler). Two effects multiply: three times smaller time steps (102 instead of 35 sweeps) and about 5.5 times more volumes
  (see the AMR section above), i.e. a factor of 16 in the best case. Local time stepping would help here, as the vast majority of the volumes could use a three times larger time step.

<div style="background-color: #fcc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
  The Finite Differences with lop-sided advection deadlock with the default task graph compiler `fusion` if more than one thread is used
  (all threads of the runs with 2, 4 and 8 threads stood still without a single time step; with one thread the same
  executable makes progress). The sequential compiler (`--compiler sequential`) runs fine. See the profiling section below for the
  diagnosis. The first run of the scheme study consequently hit the timeout for these two schemes.
</div>

The runs do not write checkpoints. To compare solution profiles in addition (lapse over radius), pass
`--extra-arguments "--checkpoint-interval 1"` and extract the profiles with probe-single-black-hole.py.

Fourth-order Finite Differences with AMR will be added to this study once the corresponding
branch is merged.


## Performance study

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 studies-single-black-hole.py performance --variant profile --threads 1 2 4 8 16 --repetitions 3
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

runs the Finite Volumes on the regular mesh and with one AMR level (regular, amr-1; radius 1) for each task graph compiler
(`sequential`, `fusion-only`, `producer-consumer-sequential`, `fusion`) and every thread count.
The thread count is set via `OMP_NUM_THREADS` and therefore applies to OpenMP builds only.
The profile variant uses Peano's stats build. The study measures the wall time of each run (`results-performance.csv`).

Things to compare:

### Outcome on the profile mesh

Wall time of the whole run in seconds (setup, i.e. TwoPunctures and mesh construction, plus 35 time steps on the regular mesh
or 102 on the adaptive mesh), eight-core workstation, Finite Volumes, profile variant. The regular mesh figures are the mean of two runs (they differ by less than 0.5 s), the
adaptive mesh figures stem from one run.

| Mesh | Compiler | 1 thread | 4 threads | 8 threads | Speedup 1 to 8 |
| ---- | -------- | -------: | --------: | --------: | -------------: |
| regular | sequential | 39.0 | 14.5 | 12.1 | 3.2 |
| regular | fusion-only | 43.8 | 13.4 | 9.4 | 4.7 |
| regular | producer-consumer-sequential | 39.4 | 14.5 | 12.4 | 3.2 |
| regular | fusion | 45.3 | 12.2 | 9.5 | 4.8 |
| adaptive (1 level, radius 1) | sequential | 511 | | 172 | 3.0 |
| adaptive (1 level, radius 1) | fusion-only | 584 | | 130 | 4.5 |
| adaptive (1 level, radius 1) | producer-consumer-sequential | 504 | | 147 | 3.4 |
| adaptive (1 level, radius 1) | fusion | 592 | | 105 | 5.6 |

- The assumption that the task-based variant is the fastest holds with a qualification. The variant that combines
  fusion of the sweeps with tasks (`fusion`) is the fastest as soon as more than a handful of threads are used: 1.3 times faster than the
  sequential compiler on the regular mesh and 1.6 times faster on the adaptive mesh with eight threads. It is, however, the slowest variant with
  one thread (15% slower on both meshes), i.e. it pays an overhead that only parallelism recovers.
- The task-based variant without fusion (`producer-consumer-sequential`) is no faster than the sequential
  compiler on the regular mesh (12.4 s vs. 12.1 s) and only 17% faster on the adaptive mesh. The gain comes from fusing sweeps, not from tasking:
  `fusion-only` (no tasks) matches `fusion` on the regular mesh (9.4 s vs. 9.5 s). Tasks gain another 24% on the adaptive mesh
  (105 s vs. 130 s), where the patches are costlier and the tasks have more to overlap.
- Scalability is poor on all variants. Even the best variants reach a parallel efficiency of only 60% (regular) to 70% (adaptive)
  on eight threads. On the regular mesh the setup (TwoPunctures plus mesh construction) takes 2.8 s of the 9.5 s, and it does not benefit from the choice of the compiler.
- The adaptive mesh costs 11 times more than the regular mesh at eight threads with `fusion`.

For the compute-bound view of the kernels, see @ref benchmarks_exagrype_kernel_benchmarks.


## Profile of the code and recommendations

We profiled the profile variant with Intel VTune (2024.3, hotspot analysis with software sampling, i.e. no hardware counters are needed) on the
eight-core workstation. The Peano build is the one described above, with `-g -O3`. A profile looks like

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
source /opt/intel/oneapi/vtune/2024.3/vtune-vars.sh
cd studies-performance/regular-fusion          # a directory produced by studies-single-black-hole.py
OMP_NUM_THREADS=8 vtune -collect hotspots -knob sampling-mode=sw -result-dir vtune-regular-fusion -- ./single-black-hole
vtune -report summary  -result-dir vtune-regular-fusion
vtune -report hotspots -result-dir vtune-regular-fusion -limit 25
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The percentages below refer to CPU time summed over all threads. We profiled five setups:

| Setup | CPU time [s] | Remarks |
| ----- | -----------: | ------- |
| FV, regular mesh, `fusion`, 1 thread | 47 | cleanest view on the kernels |
| FV, regular mesh, `fusion`, 8 threads | 66 | spin time 3.1 s, overhead 3.5 s |
| FV, regular mesh, `sequential`, 8 threads | 86 | spin time 6.9 s, overhead 7.5 s |
| FV, adaptive mesh (1 level), `fusion`, 8 threads | 837 | spin 40 s, overhead 47 s |
| FD4 (RK4), regular mesh, `fusion`, 8 threads | 477 | spin 35 s, overhead 38 s |

### Where the time goes

**Finite Volumes, one thread.** The three CCZ4 routines plus the generated Rusanov kernel make up 63% of the time: `exagrype::ccz4::ncp`
(24%), the generated kernel `CCZ4_timeStepWithRusanov` itself (23%, the `ncp` implementation is partially inlined into it),
`exagrype::ccz4::source` (12%), `nonconservativeProduct` (3%). TwoPunctures (`PunctEvalAtArbitPositionFaster`, the initial data)
takes 18% of this short run. Heap management takes 8%: `free` 4.3%, `operator new` 2.2%, `memset` 1.2%. `memcpy` takes 2.4%.
Peano's mesh traversal (`peano4::grid`) is below 2%.

**Finite Volumes, eight threads.** The compute kernels shrink to 39% (they scale), whereas everything that moves data does not:
`memcpy` grows from 1.2 s to 5.1 s of CPU time (8%) and `peano4::grid` takes 3.1 s with `fusion` but 7.9 s (9%) with the sequential compiler.
That is the effect that makes fusion fast: it cuts the traversal cost by 60%. The remaining 6 s of spinning at barriers
(`__kmp_fork_barrier`) and the serial setup limit the speedup.

**Adaptive mesh.** Compute kernels dominate even more (`CCZ4_timeStepWithRusanov` 17%, `source` 13%, `nonconservativeProduct` 4%),
but `memcpy` (8%), TwoPunctures (5%, the new volumes need their initial data), `getVertexType` (4%) and allocation
(`free`, `memset`, `new`, `memalign`: together 3%) still take a combined 20%. The setup takes 8.7 s of the 105 s.

**Fourth-order Finite Differences.** `memcpy` is the biggest single item with 14% (65 s), followed by `ncp` (13%), `source` (10%),
`peano4::grid::GridTraversalEventGenerator::getVertexType` (6%, an event generator which should cost nothing)
and the right-hand side kernel itself (4%). Spin and overhead time account for another 15% (73 s).

### Task-based deadlock (needs a fix before anything else)

The Finite Differences with lop-sided advection (`--lopsided`) deadlock with the compilers that spawn tasks as soon as OpenMP
uses more than one thread. We attached `gdb` to a hanging run with eight threads: all eight threads sit inside
`tarch::multicore::omp::internal::hasTaskFinishedInOpenMP5()` (`src/tarch/multicore/omp/Tasks.cpp`), called from the
body of a task created in `spawnTaskWithOpenMP5()`. That function polls the task's input dependencies in a busy loop *inside the task body*
without ever yielding. If every thread executes a task that waits for a predecessor which still sits in the task queue,
nobody is left to run the predecessor. The lop-sided advection adds an action set `AddLopsidedAdvection_WaitForTask` to the task graph (the plain FD4 scheme has none), which
presumably creates the dependent chains that trigger this situation; we did not analyse the dependency structure further. With one thread, or with the sequential compiler, the
same code runs through. We consider this a bug of the OpenMP 5 fallback (the compiler offers OpenMP 5, so the OpenMP 6 dependency path is not used).

### Recommendations

Sorted by expected benefit per effort; we did not change any code in Peano.

1. **Fix the polling loop of the OpenMP 5 task fallback.** Add an `#pragma omp taskyield` to the loop that polls `hasTaskFinishedInOpenMP5()` in
   `spawnTaskWithOpenMP5()` (the polling loop for the in/out dependency a few lines above it already has one) or defer the task until its predecessors have finished.
   Until then, run the Finite Differences with lop-sided advection with `--compiler sequential`. We did not test the taskyield patch.
2. **Use `fusion` for runs with at least four threads and `sequential` for a single thread** (see the table above). The default of the script
   is `fusion`, which is the right choice for the production runs but not for debugging on a single core.
3. **Remove the heap temporaries from the generated Finite Volume kernel.** The kernel generated from the DSL
   (`kernels/CCZ4_rusanov3d.cpph`) allocates 30 temporary arrays per call, each as an array of pointers plus one `new double[...]` per cell of the batch, and
   deletes them again. For CCZ4 with 59 unknowns, this costs 8% of the single-thread time (plus the `memset`s that initialise them). Reusing
   thread-local scratch buffers, or fusing the loops so that the lambda and flux fields stay in registers, removes that. It lives in
   `RusanovWithExplicitEuler.py` and the DSL back end, i.e. in the code generator and not in the benchmark.
4. **Reduce what the kernels compute.** `ncp` (24%) and `source` (12%) are evaluated three times (once per face direction) on averaged states, and `maxEigenvalue` is evaluated six times per volume
   (three times on the halo extent for the Rusanov flux and three times on the result for the next time step). The latter is a cheap
   routine for CCZ4, but the former two are the most expensive in the profile. Check if their arithmetic vectorises (use the optimisation report as
   described in @ref benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_single_black_hole_implementation); we did not run a micro-architecture analysis.
5. **Avoid the data copies.** `memcpy` takes 8% on eight threads for the Finite Volumes and 14% for the Finite Differences, and it is the one item
   that scales worst with the number of threads (it is probably limited by memory bandwidth). The call stacks show that both for the Finite Volumes and for the
   Finite Differences, the copies come from `toolbox::blockstructured::api::ReconstructPatchWithHalo` (its `touchCellFirstTime()`):
   in every time step (every Runge-Kutta stage for FD4), each cell copies its patch and the halo of its neighbours
   into a temporary array before the kernel runs. Reading the halo straight from the face data inside the kernel, or reusing the reconstructed patch between the stages, would save bandwidth.
   We did not look into how the copy is implemented in detail.
6. **Make the initial data cheaper.** TwoPunctures costs 8 to 9 CPU seconds even on the profile mesh and is evaluated for every volume,
   including every volume that adaptive refinement creates. A single, non-spinning black hole is spherically symmetric, so a table of the metric along
   the radius (computed once) and an interpolation is enough in principle. This matters for the profile variant and for AMR runs with frequent
   re-initialisation; it is a one-off cost for the production run. We did not check that the interpolation retains the accuracy that the benchmark requires.
7. **Investigate the size of the refined region and use local time stepping.** The refined region is much larger than the radius suggests (see above),
   and all volumes then use the smallest time step. The adaptive run does 3 times more sweeps for 5.5 times more volumes, i.e. it spends
   about 16 times the compute of the regular run on a problem where only the area close to the puncture needs the resolution.
   Since we only profiled one AMR level, we expect the effect to be much worse for the two or three levels that the production setup will need.
8. **Reduce the serial part.** The setup (TwoPunctures plus mesh construction, 2.8 s of 9.5 s on the regular mesh) is a fixed cost, as long as it
   does not scale: it is the main reason for the 60% parallel efficiency. Part of it (TwoPunctures) is item 6.
9. **Look at `getVertexType`** in the Finite Difference profile (6% of 477 CPU seconds for a function that classifies vertices): with eight threads it competes with the
   kernels; presumably it is called once per vertex and per Runge-Kutta stage. A cached classification would remove it. We did not verify the call count.

The VTune results are not part of the repository. The command lines above reproduce them.


## Slurm scripts

The directory slurm-scripts holds examples for a cluster with Slurm (the module names follow
Hamilton at Durham; adapt the partitions, the modules and the path to your Peano checkout through `PEANO`):

| Script | Purpose |
| ------ | ------- |
| schemes.slm | The scheme study on one node. |
